# Summary of changes for run bec8ccf1-207a-44a1-bdbe-d128ae274dd2
I wrote all five deliverables at the project root, committed and pushed them. Two things are untested: no LaTeX compiler was available, so `RESEARCH_PAPER.tex` has not been compiled, and matplotlib was not available, so the three plotting scripts have only been syntax-checked.

The articles and paper stand on their own. They contain no mention of the proof tooling, file paths or identifier names. Each one says plainly that \(N^{1/5}\) is the best *known* deterministic cost, not a proven lower bound for factoring, and that the point \((\tfrac13,\tfrac13)\) remains unconstructed.

- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,500 words): a popular-science account. It covers the three-term cost \(E(a,b)=\max(a,b,\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b)\) and why its minimum is \(\tfrac15\), with that minimum reached only at \(a=b=\tfrac15\). It then walks through the three independent arguments for the same \(\tfrac15\): the slack-constraint lemma, the delete-a-term argument (optimum \(\gamma/(1+u+w/c)\), with benefit-to-cost ratio \(w/c\le1\)), and the space–time invariant \(3A+2C\ge1\). Finally it covers the divisibility-covering exit, the counting wall \(4^n\le(2n+1)M^{|S||T|}\), the two constructed ends of that wall, and the open zero-slack point \((\tfrac13,\tfrac13)\).
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 4,600 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`**: matching papers with an abstract, a summary theorem, and every definition, lemma and theorem stated in full with proof sketches. They include algorithms, labelled computational illustrations, a discussion of the five screening lemmas, and future directions. A script check confirmed that the braces, environments and math delimiters in the `.tex` file are balanced.
- **`demo.py`**: a standard-library script that runs in under a second and checks each result numerically. All checks pass. It ends with an exploratory search for the smallest \(k\times k\) coverings, labelled as exploration rather than proof. That search gives \(M_1(n)=\operatorname{lcm}(1..n)\) for \(n\le5\), \(M_2(n)=\max(n,3)\) for \(n\le8\) followed by a jump to \(M_2(9)=18\), and \(M_3(n)=\max(n,5)\) for \(n\le17\).
